[
  "[SCENE: 01. Starting Soon (hold on Starting Soon card for voiceover intro).]\nOn the podcast No Dogs in Space, Marcus Parks coined a phrase while breaking down the German band Can.\nHe called it \"The Sound Above.\"\nIt means art, music, or ideas that deliberately exist one tier higher than an audience's current level of comfort and comprehension.\n\nIt isn't noise. It has a pulse you can follow.\nBut it forces your brain to stretch until the underlying structure clicks.\n\n[SCENE: Switch to 02. Monologue / Full Camera.]\n[PAUSE]\nWelcome to The Sound Above. I'm Mike Hall.\n\nIn 2008, the comfortable default for enterprise software was drag-and-drop wizards and click-to-generate code.\nA small group of developers in Chicago started meeting on the second Wednesday of the month in the Sears Tower to pursue something harder: Test-Driven Development, domain modeling, and open-source tooling.\nTo the enterprise mainstream, it sounded unnatural and overly strict.\nIt was the sound above.\n\n[PAUSE]\nFor Episode 1, we pull out the very first interview I ever recorded on video: November 19, 2011, at Software Craftsmanship North America at the Swissotel in downtown Chicago, with Sergio Pereira, the co-founder of Chicago Alt.NET.\n\nThree years before I flipped open a laptop in that hotel hallway to film Sergio in Photo Booth, he was the guy behind the camera filming my very first user group presentation.\n\nIn an era of instant AI code generation, we are putting the original 2011 tape right beside the recovered code, git receipts, and primary records to examine what early software craftsmanship looks like when you look past the nostalgia.\n\nLet's pull up the tape and follow the evidence.\n",
  "[SCENE: Camera. Keep the Chicago Alt.NET Wayback archive nearby.]\nRight at the top of the recording, I ask Sergio a plain question: what is Chicago Alt.NET?\nNotice how he replies.\nHe turns down any neat ideological label.\n\nRather than a slogan, he talks about the people in the room and what they were trying to do.\nThey were working developers who wrote .NET every day, liked .NET, and wanted to level up their craft and the quality of their code.\n\nIn April 2007, David Laribee wrote the Alt.NET Manifesto, and that October developers gathered in Austin for the first Alt.NET Open Spaces.\nSergio launched Chicago Alt.NET in late 2007 with Derik Whittaker and John Nuechterlein (jdn), and later ran it with Eduardo Scoz.\n\n[PAUSE]\nWhat stands out when I hear it now is how much practical planning mattered.\nThey met on the second Wednesday of every month at 6:30 PM at Redpoint in the Sears Tower.\nRedpoint gave the group the room and bought the pizza every month.\n\nAnd 6:30 PM was a deliberate middle ground: not too late for people working in the Loop, and early enough for suburban commuters catching the 8:30 PM Metra train home.\n\nA typical night drew around twenty-five people.\nSergio points out that twenty-five is an ideal room for someone who has never given a user group talk before.\nHe encouraged members to take a talk they had already shared with their team at work and bring it to the group.\n\n[SHOW: November 2008 chicagoalt.net Wayback snapshot.]\nI was living proof of that charter.\nOn November 12, 2008, only three months after my first Chicago Alt.NET meeting, I got up at Redpoint and gave my first user group talk on the NHaml view engine, right alongside Adam Tybor talking about Spark.\n[FREEFORM: Talk about what it felt like to prepare and give that first NHaml talk in a room of twenty-five peers.]\n",
  "[SCENE: Camera, then split screen with the archive timeline and git log.]\nThe question I want to follow in this rewatch is clear.\nWhat made Chicago Alt.NET a room people kept coming back to, and how did it connect to the wider Chicago developer community?\n\nSergio points out the group was never only about .NET. It was about software development.\nHe names NHibernate, CodeSharp, IronRuby, TDD, BDD, and Kanban as ideas that needed a real venue.\n\nIn his April 2009 Wrox book, Ruby on Rails for Microsoft Developers, Antonio Cangiano noted how Alt.NET developers looking to Castle MonoRail and Rails helped nudge Microsoft from WebForms toward ASP.NET MVC and bundling jQuery in Visual Studio.\n\n[PAUSE]\nWhile prepping for today, three archival finds lined up right with what Sergio mentions on tape.\n\nFirst, around the eleven-minute mark, Sergio talks about trying open formats and fishbowl debates.\nIn my March 12, 2009 blog recap, I wrote about one of those four-chair Fishbowl nights, with Adam Tybor leading a debate on Domain-Driven Design and Sergio running a second circle on the db4o object database until nine at night.\n\nNext, that spring, Sergio joked on Twitter that retweeting Scott Hanselman made no real impact because everyone following us already followed Scott.\nI disagreed, and in the spirit of that group, I built an ASP.NET MVC web app called twatio.com to measure unique follower overlap with real data.\nYears later, twatio.com pointed right over to ugtastic.com.\n\n[SHOW: Chicago Code Camp just3ws/ccs commit log and Signatory Network Map.]\nThird, listen at ten minutes and twenty-nine seconds when Sergio mentions volunteering and helping organize Chicago Code Camp so it would go beyond .NET.\nI recently dug up the 2010 to 2012 Ruby on Rails repo, just3ws/ccs, that ran chicagocodecamp.com, and checked it against the Software Craftsmanship Manifesto ledger.\n\nSergio signed the Manifesto in 2009 as Signatory 592 from Grayslake, Illinois, right where Chicago Code Camp met at the College of Lake County.\nAnd in the git history of our Rails app, Sergio Pereira is the number two contributor with twenty-four commits in Ruby, Haml, and Sass alongside my 764 commits, and he spoke at the May 2012 camp.\n\nHe didn't merely talk about crossing language boundaries.\nHe rolled up his sleeves as a volunteer and co-organizer, writing Ruby on Rails code with us to run a free community conference.\n[FREEFORM: Reflect on rediscovering Sergio's 24 commits in the Chicago Code Camp Rails repo and his #592 Manifesto signature.]\n",
  "[SCENE: Switch to archive tape in Scene 03.]\n[REWATCH: Sergio Pereira interview (YouTube qOHdZKz1WFw), start at 00:00.]\nLet's play the 2011 conversation with Sergio Pereira.\nLet his description of the group carry the story.\n[FREEFORM: React to the tape naturally. Pause when a specific moment or phrase stands out.]\nWatch for these turns as the recording plays:\nAt 00:00, why Sergio resists defining Alt.NET and focuses on continuous improvement.\nAt 02:19, the second Wednesday at 6:30 PM at Redpoint in the Sears Tower and the suburban train commute.\nAt 03:06, the twenty-five-person room and his charter to cultivate speakers from inside the community.\nAt 05:17, my thank-you to Sergio for the room where I gave my first user group talk, and his memories of starting the group in late 2007 with Derik Whittaker and John Nuechterlein (jdn).\nAt 07:09, giving working developers a chance to see TDD, BDD, and tools from other platforms and judge for themselves.\nAt 10:29, cross-platform regulars, Kanban, and participating in Chicago Code Camp.\nAt 11:42, Redpoint's pizza sponsorship, trying open projector nights and fishbowl debates, and curating speakers with Eduardo Scoz compared to ChicagoRuby.\n[SCENE: Return to camera after the tape finishes.]\n[FREEFORM: Immediate reaction: what sounded different on the tape today compared to how you remembered it?]\n",
  "[SCENE: Return to 02. Monologue / Full Camera for wrap-up and synthesis.]\nHaving the full transcript open right beside this recording changes how I hear it.\nFor a long time, I doubted these two hundred interviews would ever be transcribed.\n\nIn 2014, I built a repo called many-hands-make-light-work to crowd-transcribe them with the community, because paid cloud audio APIs were way too pricey and manual typing was too much for one person.\n\nIt took building my five-Raspberry-Pi whisper.cpp Fluffle cluster in 2023, and now running local Apple Silicon transcription in my media pipeline, to turn that 2011 Photo Booth recording into a verified forty-four-turn transcript with thirteen chapters.\n\n[PAUSE]\nWhen I read Sergio's line 145 in the transcript, his point holds up fifteen years later:\n\"That's the place that they can come, see those things, and judge for themselves if it's going to work for them or not.\"\n\nIn 2008, the default vendor path was drag-and-drop WebForms wizards that hid HTTP and made unit testing painful.\nToday, the default path is often generative AI tooling that can output hundreds of lines of convincing code without revealing the domain boundaries underneath.\n\nThe takeaway from Chicago Alt.NET is grounded and durable.\nTools turn over every few years, and no vendor default replaces your engineering judgment.\nWe still need rooms where working developers can demo what they are building, look beyond their own stack, and verify whether a new tool holds up in practice.\n[FREEFORM: Compare what you remembered with what the transcript and archival records show side by side.]\n",
  "[SCENE: Outro card with Sergio Pereira's name and the episode link.]\nWhen you listen to Can's Tago Mago or Miles Davis's Bitches Brew for the first time, it doesn't meet you on your terms. You have to meet it on its terms.\n\nThe same thing happened with Chicago Alt.NET.\nIt wasn't built for easy corporate consumption.\nIt was a room where working developers challenged the defaults of their day and made space for each other to learn.\n\nThank you to Sergio Pereira for building that room, for writing code with us on Chicago Code Camp, and for recording that inaugural interview fifteen years ago.\n\nIf you were in those rooms at ThoughtWorks or Redpoint in the Sears Tower, or if you remember the friction of giving your first talk when everything felt unfamiliar, share that memory in the comments below.\n\nIn Episode 2, Ray Hightower joins us to explore ChicagoRuby and how to build rooms that welcome developers across platforms without lowering the standard of craft.\n\nInteractive transcripts, primary git logs, and show notes are at just3ws.com.\nUntil next time, keep your feedback loops tight, take care of each other, and keep making room to learn.\n"
]
